<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ctesiphon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ctesiphon was a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> River in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, whose great vaulted palace hall, the Taq Kasra, still partly stands. Captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in the 630s, it declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> founded Baghdad nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ctesiphon.jpg" alt="Ctesiphon" width="296" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renato de carvalho ferreira · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ctesiphon_map-en_reformat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
